```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc3
diagnosis_text:
PC3 (10.3.0.100/24) has a misconfigured default gateway. Its routing table
shows "default via 10.3.0.99 dev eth0", but 10.3.0.99 does not exist anywhere
in the Office B VLAN (or anywhere in the topology). The real gateway for the
10.3.0.0/24 VLAN is 10.3.0.1, which is the VRRP virtual IP shared between
dswb1 (br0.10, 10.3.0.2/24 + 10.3.0.1/32) and dswb2 (br0.10, 10.3.0.3/24).
This VIP is fully operational and ARP-reachable from PC3's segment.

The failure mechanism is straightforward: traffic within the local /24 uses
on-link forwarding (no gateway involved), so intra-VLAN reachability is
unaffected. Any packet destined outside 10.3.0.0/24 — the Internet, Office A
subnets, the Server Room — requires the kernel to send it via the default route.
The kernel attempts to ARP-resolve 10.3.0.99; that request goes unanswered
(ARP cache shows the entry as "incomplete"), so every off-subnet packet is
silently dropped before it ever reaches the switch. No other Office B host is
affected because they all correctly point to 10.3.0.1.

The symptom appeared "after a desktop reconfiguration" because someone
manually (or via a mis-applied network profile) changed PC3's static gateway
entry from 10.3.0.1 to the nonexistent 10.3.0.99.

Root cause: PC3's default gateway is set to a nonexistent address (10.3.0.99)
instead of the correct VRRP VIP (10.3.0.1).

Corrective fix: On PC3, delete the current default route and add the correct
one:
  ip route del default
  ip route add default via 10.3.0.1 dev eth0
To make it persistent, update /etc/network/interfaces (or the relevant
NetworkManager/netplan config) so the gateway is 10.3.0.1.
=== END ===
```